Now I have all the detail I need. Here is the complete implementation plan.

---

## Implementation Plan: `render_uploaded_image()` helper with `<picture>` / WebP fallback

### Overview

The plan has four concrete deliverables:

1. Add `render_uploaded_image()` to `config/functions.php` (the right home — see rationale below).
2. Update 7 view locations to call the helper.
3. Write `scripts/backfill-webp.php` for existing uploads.
4. Update the PHP rules documentation (`CLAUDE.md` / `.claude/rules/php.md`).

---

## Part 1 — Helper placement decision

**Choose Option B: `config/functions.php`.**

Reasoning from the codebase:

- `inc/lib/func.php` contains infra-level PHP 4-era procedural utilities (`location()`, `error()`, `imageUpload()`, `imageDelete()`). Its functions operate on disk paths, globals, and side-effects. It runs in both the public front-end boot and the legacy admin pages boot. Its `e()` is not even present there — it lives in `config/functions.php`.
- `config/functions.php` is the layer that holds all presentation helpers (`e()`, `ed()`, `dump()`, `dd()`, `img_path()`, `lord_icon()`, `base_path()`). Every one of these returns a string and is pure output. `render_uploaded_image()` is exactly that category: it takes a URL string, returns HTML markup, uses `htmlspecialchars`.
- The project rule in `.claude/rules/php.md` explicitly calls `e()` the escaping helper — `render_uploaded_image()` wraps `e()` internally, so it belongs in the same file.
- `config/functions.php` is loaded for every request via the autoload chain (`config/setup.php` → `config/project.php`). `inc/lib/func.php` is loaded slightly later in `base.class.php`. Putting the helper in `config/functions.php` makes it available at the same time as `e()`, `base_path()`, etc.

---

## Part 2 — The helper function

Add to the **end** of `/c/Users/edn_y/renew_nineonelabs/config/functions.php`, before the `require_once __DIR__ . '/hreflang.php';` line:

```php
if (!function_exists('render_uploaded_image')) {
    /**
     * Render an uploaded image as <picture> with WebP-first fallback.
     *
     * - Empty $src          → returns ''
     * - .svg / .ico         → plain <img> (not convertible)
     * - jpg/jpeg/png/gif    → <picture><source type="image/webp"><img></picture>
     *
     * No disk I/O is performed. Trusts that makeWebpSibling() ran at upload time.
     * The browser falls back to the <img src> silently if the .webp is absent.
     *
     * @param string $src     Web URL of the original image (may be empty)
     * @param string $alt     Alt text (will be HTML-escaped)
     * @param string $class   CSS class string applied to <img> (will be HTML-escaped)
     * @param string $loading loading="lazy"|"eager"|"" — omitted from markup when empty
     * @return string         Ready-to-echo HTML markup
     */
    function render_uploaded_image(
        string $src,
        string $alt,
        string $class = '',
        string $loading = ''
    ): string {
        if ($src === '') {
            return '';
        }

        $escapedSrc     = htmlspecialchars($src,     ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8', false);
        $escapedAlt     = htmlspecialchars($alt,     ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8', false);
        $classAttr      = $class   !== '' ? ' class="'   . htmlspecialchars($class,   ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8', false) . '"' : '';
        $loadingAttr    = $loading !== '' ? ' loading="' . htmlspecialchars($loading, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8', false) . '"' : '';

        // SVG and ICO are not converted; output a plain <img>
        $ext = strtolower((string) pathinfo($src, PATHINFO_EXTENSION));
        if ($ext === 'svg' || $ext === 'ico') {
            return '<img src="' . $escapedSrc . '" alt="' . $escapedAlt . '"' . $classAttr . $loadingAttr . '>';
        }

        // Derive the WebP sibling URL by swapping the extension
        $webpSrc     = preg_replace('/\.[^.]+$/', '.webp', $src);
        $escapedWebp = htmlspecialchars((string) $webpSrc, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8', false);

        return '<picture>'
            . '<source srcset="' . $escapedWebp . '" type="image/webp">'
            . '<img src="' . $escapedSrc . '" alt="' . $escapedAlt . '"' . $classAttr . $loadingAttr . '>'
            . '</picture>';
    }
}
```

**Design notes:**

- `ENT_QUOTES | false` for `double_encode=false` matches the exact contract of the existing `e()` helper in this file (line 98–100).
- `preg_replace('/\.[^.]+$/', '.webp', $src)` mirrors the identical expression in `ImageProcessor::makeWebpSibling()` (line 29) and `deleteWebpSibling()` (line 61), so the URL derivation is consistent with the disk path derivation.
- The function is wrapped in `if (!function_exists(...))` matching the pattern of every other function in this file.
- No `global` access, no disk I/O. Pure string manipulation.

---

## Part 3 — View updates (7 locations)

### Important notes before the diff

- In `views/pages/works/view.php` and `views/components/work-modal.php`, `$thumbnailImage` is **already HTML-escaped** at assignment (`$thumbnailImage = e(...)`). Since `render_uploaded_image()` performs its own escaping internally, you must pass the **raw** value. The raw value is `(string) ($work['thumbnailImage'] ?? '')`.
- For locations where `e($var)` is used inline in the `<img src>`, the raw `$var` is passed to `render_uploaded_image()`.
- `$alt` and `$class` values are passed raw (unescaped) to `render_uploaded_image()` since it escapes them internally.
- `loading=""` is omitted (empty string default) for all existing images, preserving current browser-default behaviour.

---

### Location 1 — `views/pages/index.php:459` — Portfolio grid thumbnail

**Context:** Inside a `<figure class="no-sub-gallery-item__img">` element.

**Before:**
```php
<figure class="no-sub-gallery-item__img">
    <img src="<?= e((string) ($work['thumbnailImage'] ?? '')) ?>"
        alt="<?= e($workLabel) ?>">
</figure>
```

**After:**
```php
<figure class="no-sub-gallery-item__img">
    <?= render_uploaded_image(
        (string) ($work['thumbnailImage'] ?? ''),
        (string) ($workLabel ?? ''),
        '',
        'lazy'
    ) ?>
</figure>
```

Note: `loading="lazy"` is appropriate here — the portfolio grid is a below-the-fold list. Confirm with the team whether the first viewport item needs `eager`; if so, use a conditional based on loop index.

---

### Location 2 — `views/pages/works/view.php:124` — Hero thumbnail image

**Context:** The `else` branch when there is no `$thumbnailVideo`.

**Before:**
```php
<?php else: ?>
<img src="<?= $thumbnailImage ?>" alt="<?= $title ?>">
<?php endif; ?>
```

**After:**
```php
<?php else: ?>
<?= render_uploaded_image(
    (string) ($work['thumbnailImage'] ?? ''),
    $title,
    '',
    'eager'
) ?>
<?php endif; ?>
```

Note: Hero image uses `loading="eager"` (it is the above-the-fold LCP element). `$thumbnailImage` at line 59 was pre-escaped with `e()`; we use the raw source array instead.

---

### Location 3 — `views/pages/works/view.php:144` — Detail images loop

**Before:**
```php
<?php foreach ($detailImages as $detailImage): ?>
<img src="<?= e((string) $detailImage) ?>" alt="<?= $title ?>">
<?php endforeach; ?>
```

**After:**
```php
<?php foreach ($detailImages as $detailImage): ?>
<?= render_uploaded_image(
    (string) $detailImage,
    $title,
    '',
    'lazy'
) ?>
<?php endforeach; ?>
```

---

### Location 4 — `views/components/work-modal.php:103` — Modal hero thumbnail

**Before:**
