## Potential pitfalls and resolutions

**Double-escaping on `$thumbnailImage` in `view.php` and `work-modal.php`.**
Both files assign `$thumbnailImage = e((string) ($work['thumbnailImage'] ?? ''))` at the top. If you pass `$thumbnailImage` directly to `render_uploaded_image()`, the URL will be double-escaped (e.g., `&` → `&amp;amp;`). Always use `(string) ($work['thumbnailImage'] ?? '')` (the raw source array) at Locations 2 and 4. This is the same pattern used at Locations 3 and 5 which already pass `$detailImage` raw from the `$detailImages` array.

**`preg_replace` on a URL with query strings.**
`buildAssetUrl()` in `api/works.php` produces URLs like `/uploads/works/abc123.jpg` (no query string, `rawurlencode` is applied to the filename). External URLs (`https://...`) pass through unchanged. The extension regex `/\.[^.]+$/` is safe for these forms. If a URL ever has a query string like `/uploads/works/abc.jpg?v=123`, the regex would strip `?v=123` along with `.jpg` — but this cannot happen with the current upload system (no cache-busting on upload URLs).

**`BASE_PATH` prefix for sub-directory deployments.**
`buildAssetUrl()` already calls `base_path()` which prepends `BASE_PATH` (e.g. `/www`). The URLs that arrive in `$thumbnailImage` / `$thumb` are already fully resolved web paths. `render_uploaded_image()` receives them as-is and performs no further path manipulation, so sub-directory deployments work correctly without any changes to the helper.

